\subsection{Phase F: constructive stochastic practical stability}
\label{sec:stability-widening-phase-f}

The stochastic theorem in Sec.~\ref{sec:finite-live-capture} intentionally does
not infer a Gaussian drift inequality from deterministic bounded-input ISS.
With the explicit Phase-B metric and Phase-E deterministic decrease margin, the
missing drift coefficients can instead be derived from conditional noise
moments and a source-map remainder bound.  The argument below does not require a
zero conditional mean after innovation gating: accepted/rejected branches may
depend on the current sensor realization.

On a certified Phase-C normal-Live tube, write one exact accepted/rejected
source step as
\begin{equation}
 \vct z_{k+1}
 =\vct f_k(\vct z_k)
 +\vct y_k,
 \qquad
 \vct y_k:=\mat G_k\vct w_k
 +\vct r_{w,k}(\vct z_k,\vct w_k),
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-map}
\end{equation}
where $\vct f_k$ is the zero-noise nonlinear map already covered by Phase E,
$\mat G_k\vct w_k$ is the first-order noise channel on the realized source
branch, and the remaining noise--geometry interaction satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \|\vct r_{w,k}\|
 \le c_{zw}\|\vct z_k\|\,\|\vct w_k\|
      +c_{ww}\|\vct w_k\|^2.
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-remainder}
\end{equation}
Such constants exist on every compact accepted-measurement/geodesic source
family because the rotation, gain injection, and reset maps are smooth on each
source branch.  Rejected-update branches are included in the same uniform
bounds.  Assume the pre-gate sensor/process increment has conditional covariance
bounded by
\begin{equation}
 \mathbb E[\vct w_k\vct w_k^\top\mid\mathcal F_k]
 =\mat\Sigma_k,
 \qquad
 \mat\Sigma_k\preceq\overline{\mat\Sigma}.
 \label{eq:widen-gaussian-noise}
\end{equation}
For conditionally Gaussian pre-gate noise define
\begin{align}
 s_2&:=\operatorname{tr}(\overline{\mat\Sigma}),\\
 s_4&:=s_2^2+2\operatorname{tr}(\overline{\mat\Sigma}^{\,2}),
 \label{eq:widen-gaussian-moments}
\end{align}
so that
$\mathbb E[\|w_k\|^2\mid\mathcal F_k]\le s_2$ and
$\mathbb E[\|w_k\|^4\mid\mathcal F_k]\le s_4$.  The proof uses only these
moment inequalities, so the same theorem applies to non-Gaussian noise when
corresponding uniform second- and fourth-moment bounds are supplied.

Let
$L_f:=\bar A+\ell$, $\bar G:=\sup_k\|\mat G_k\|$, and let $a_0$ be
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-a0}.  Define
\begin{equation}
 c_0:=\bar G\sqrt{s_2}+c_{ww}s_2,
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-c0}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{align}
 a_s&:=\frac{a_0}{2}
 -2p_+^{\rm an}L_f c_{zw}\sqrt{s_2}
 -3p_+^{\rm an}c_{zw}^2s_2,
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-margin}\\
 b_s&:=3p_+^{\rm an}\bar G^2s_2
 +3p_+^{\rm an}c_{ww}^2s_4
 +\frac{2[p_+^{\rm an}L_fc_0]^2}{a_0}.
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-floor}
\end{align}

\begin{theorem}[Constructive mean-square practical drift under gated noise]
\label{thm:widen-stochastic-drift}
Assume Eqs.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-map}--\eqref{eq:widen-gaussian-moments}
and $a_s>0$.  While the source state remains in the certified Phase-C
normal-Live family,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathbb E[V_{k+1}\mid\mathcal F_k]
 \le(1-\alpha_s)V_k+b_s,
 \qquad
 \alpha_s:=\frac{a_s}{p_+^{\rm an}}>0.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-drift}
\end{equation}
Consequently, over any interval on which the certified source family remains
active,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathbb E V_k
 \le(1-\alpha_s)^{k-j}\mathbb E V_j
 +\frac{b_s}{\alpha_s}.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-ms}
\end{equation}
Thus the stochastic drift coefficients previously denoted abstractly by
$(\lambda_s,\sigma_s^2)$ may be taken as
\begin{equation}
 \lambda_s=1-\alpha_s,
 \qquad \sigma_s^2=b_s,
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-coefficients}
\end{equation}
provided the explicit noise-margin condition $a_s>0$ holds.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Phase E gives
$V(f_k(z_k))-V(z_k)\le-a_0\|z_k\|^2$ and
$\|f_k(z_k)\|\le L_f\|z_k\|$.  No cancellation of the cross term is assumed.
From Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-remainder}, conditional Cauchy--Schwarz,
and the moment bounds,
\begin{align}
 \mathbb E[\|y_k\|\mid\mathcal F_k]
 &\le c_0+c_{zw}\|z_k\|\sqrt{s_2},\\
 \mathbb E[\|y_k\|^2\mid\mathcal F_k]
 &\le3\bar G^2s_2
 +3c_{zw}^2\|z_k\|^2s_2
 +3c_{ww}^2s_4.
 \label{eq:widen-y-moments}
\end{align}
Expanding $V_{k+1}$ and using $\mat P_{k+1}\preceq p_+^{\rm an}\mat I$
gives a cross term no larger than
$2p_+^{\rm an}L_f\|z_k\|\,
\mathbb E[\|y_k\|\mid\mathcal F_k]$ and a quadratic term no larger than
$p_+^{\rm an}\mathbb E[\|y_k\|^2\mid\mathcal F_k]$.
Young's inequality bounds the part
$2p_+^{\rm an}L_fc_0\|z_k\|$ by
$(a_0/2)\|z_k\|^2+2[p_+^{\rm an}L_fc_0]^2/a_0$.
Collecting terms gives
$-a_s\|z_k\|^2+b_s$.  Since
$V_k\le p_+^{\rm an}\|z_k\|^2$, Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-drift}
follows, and scalar expectation iteration gives
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-ms}.
\end{proof}

Because Gaussian noise is unbounded, a local nonlinear theorem should not
silently claim almost-sure invariance of a compact tube.  Let
$V_{\partial}>0$ be a Lyapunov level whose sublevel is contained in the
certified Phase-C normal-Live family.  A direct union bound followed by Markov's
inequality and Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-stochastic-ms} gives, for
$K:=k-j$,
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathbb P\!\left(
 \max_{j\le n\le k}V_n\ge V_{\partial}
 \right)
 \le
 \min\!\left\{1,
 \frac{\mathbb E V_j+(K+1)b_s}
      {\alpha_s V_{\partial}}\right\}.}
 \label{eq:widen-stochastic-exit}
\end{equation}
Indeed, the geometric sum of the transient terms is at most
$\mathbb E V_j/\alpha_s$, while each of the $K+1$ ultimate-bound terms is at
most $b_s/\alpha_s$.  This deliberately conservative finite-horizon bound
separates the mean-square normal-Live claim from rare excursions that invoke
the source's existing rejection, relock, and hybrid-recovery logic.

\begin{remark}[What Phase F closes]
\label{rem:widen-stochastic-scope}
Phase F removes the need to posit numerical drift coefficients independently of
the deterministic proof: $\lambda_s$ and $\sigma_s^2$ are explicit functions
of the deterministic margin, declared noise moments, and the noise--geometry
remainder constants.  It does not turn a regional MEKF into a global Gaussian
system.  It also does not assume that NIS gating preserves zero conditional
mean.  Unbounded-noise excursions are explicitly separated from the
normal-Live mean-square result and are handled by an exit probability plus the
hybrid recovery theorem rather than being hidden inside a deterministic
bounded-input assumption.
\end{remark}
